<h2>The allegation</h2>
<p>The FIR (PS Case No. 430 of 2023, Kesaria police station, East Champaran) alleged that the informant was intercepted near a graveyard. One accused struck him on the head with a sword; another cut him below the eye; the petitioner Md. Islam hit him with an iron rod; and a further accused snatched Rs. 16,000 from his pocket. The sections invoked were 341, 323, 324, 379, 307, 504, 506 and 34 of the IPC — culminating in attempt to murder.</p>

<h2>The competing cases</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Petitioners</h3><p>False implication; no motive assigned by the informant for the occurrence; the injuries are simple in nature; and the impugned order overstated their antecedents — three prior cases, not four.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>State</h3><p>The injury report shows an incised wound on the left parietal region (about 2 x half an inch, muscle deep), a lacerated wound on the left eyebrow (about 3 x half an inch, muscle deep) and a bruise on the left lower eyelid. The attack targeted vital areas including the eye, and the petitioners may abscond or tamper with evidence if protected.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The outcome</h2>
<p>The Court was not inclined to extend the privilege of anticipatory bail and rejected the application. The order is short on elaboration — as rejection orders at this stage frequently are — but the material before the Court tells its own story: weapon, target and antecedents aligned against the petitioners, and the concession on injury classification could not neutralise them.</p>

<h2>What separates grant from refusal</h2>
<p>Read alongside the same judge&rsquo;s grant of anticipatory bail the same day in a settled family assault case, this rejection maps the working boundaries of the jurisdiction under Section 482 BNSS (formerly Section 438 CrPC).</p>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Points toward protection</th><th>Points toward refusal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nature of attack</td><td>Mutual scuffle, no weapons, injuries on non-vital parts</td><td>Weapons used; blows aimed at head, eye or other vital regions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Antecedents</td><td>Clean record, settled life, community roots</td><td>Multiple prior cases, pattern of violence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relations since FIR</td><td>Genuine settlement, restored peace</td><td>Live hostility, apprehension of tampering or repetition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Investigative need</td><td>Documents-based case, nothing to recover</td><td>Weapon recoveries pending, custodial interrogation justified</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
<p>Practice point: in opposing or pressing anticipatory bail, the injury report matters more than the label attached to it. A one-line concession that injuries are &ldquo;simple&rdquo; will not help the accused when the wounds are muscle-deep and clustered around the head; conversely, prosecutors who rely on the bare section — 307 IPC — without walking the court through the medical report often lose the point. The anatomy of the injury, quite literally, decides these applications.</p>
</div>
